<workflow name="process-news">
  <description>
    Process news items from RSS feeds into a curated brief.
    Pulls items from inbox through triage, fetch, analysis, and brief creation.
  </description>

  <step id="triage">
    <description>Review inbox items and trash uninteresting ones</description>

    <precheck>
      <shell>
        count=$(ls box/inbox/news/*.news-item.card 2>/dev/null | wc -l)
        if [ "$count" -eq 0 ]; then
          exit $CHECK_SKIP
        fi
        echo "Found $count items to triage"
      </shell>
      <why>Triage reviews items in the inbox — nothing to do if inbox is empty</why>
    </precheck>

    <run>
      <agent model="haiku" max-turns="20">
        You are triaging news items in a Callback Box.

        YOUR TASK:
        Review news items in box/inbox/news/ and decide which are worth reading in full.

        STEP 0 - READ THE USER GUIDE:
        First, check if config/news-guide.news-guide.card exists. If it does, read it to understand:
        - User interests (topics they care about)
        - Disinterests (topics to skip/trash)
        - Preferences that might affect what's worth keeping

        Use this to inform your triage decisions. If no guide exists, use the default criteria below.

        STEP 1 - READ ALL ITEMS:
        Read all news items at once:
        ```
        cat box/inbox/news/*.news-item.card
        ```
        Review the title and summary of each card. Decide which to keep and which to trash.

        STEP 2 - TRASH REJECTED ITEMS:
        Use a SINGLE cb trash command with ALL rejected paths:
        ```
        cb trash box/inbox/news/item1.news-item.card box/inbox/news/item2.news-item.card ...
        ```
        cb trash accepts multiple paths in one call. Do NOT call it once per item.

        Items you want to keep stay where they are — no action needed for keeps.

        DEFAULT CRITERIA (use when no guide exists):

        INTERESTING:
        - Technical content (programming, systems, architecture)
        - Novel ideas or approaches
        - Significant news in tech/science
        - Things that would be educational or useful
        - Opinion pieces with substantive arguments

        NOT INTERESTING (trash):
        - Marketing/promotional content
        - Listicles without substance
        - Repetitive news already covered elsewhere
        - Entertainment gossip
        - Job postings, hiring announcements
        - Press releases without substance

        USER-SAVED ITEMS:
        Items with source="user" on the &lt;news-item&gt; element are explicit user picks.
        ALWAYS keep these — never trash them regardless of other criteria.

        TARGET: Keep 5-10 items. Be selective — a focused pool makes better briefs.
        If there are 30+ items, aim for the lower end. Quality over quantity.

        GUIDE-BASED CRITERIA (when guide exists):
        - Topics in &lt;interests&gt; with high/confirmed confidence → keep
        - Topics in &lt;disinterests&gt; → trash
        - Topics with "hypothesis" or "low" confidence → keep 1-2 to test, trash the rest
        - Duplicates or overlapping coverage → keep the best one, trash others
        - When in doubt, trash — there will always be more items tomorrow

        IMPORTANT: Status is expressed by location, not attributes. Don't modify the status attribute.
        Items stay in inbox/news/ if interesting, or get trashed if not.

        OUTPUT: As you process each item, state your decision:
          KEEP: [filename] - [brief reason why it's interesting]
          TRASH: [filename] - [brief reason why it's not]

        This helps track what's happening during triage.

        COMMIT (REQUIRED — do not skip):
        You MUST commit before finishing. Uncommitted work gets a generic fallback message.

        ```bash
        git add -A &amp;&amp; git commit -m "$(cat &lt;&lt;'EOF'
        Triage: &lt;kept&gt;/&lt;total&gt; items kept

        Kept:
        - &lt;title&gt; - &lt;reason&gt;

        Trashed:
        - &lt;title&gt; - &lt;reason&gt;

        Triggered-By: cb workflow
        Phase: triage
        EOF
        )"
        ```

        GIT: Do NOT add Co-Authored-By to commits. The system adds appropriate trailers automatically.
      </agent>
    </run>

    <validate severity="review">
      <shell>
        count=$(ls box/inbox/news/*.news-item.card 2>/dev/null | wc -l)
        trash=$(ls store/trash/news/*.news-item.card 2>/dev/null | wc -l)
        echo "Inbox: $count items, Trash: $trash items"
        [ "$count" -gt 0 ] || [ "$trash" -gt 0 ]
      </shell>
      <instruction>
        The git diff should show items being moved to store/trash/ via cb trash.
        Every item the agent examined should have an explicit keep or trash decision.
      </instruction>
      <why>
        Triage must make an explicit decision on every item. If items are
        skipped, they'll sit in the inbox forever and never get processed.
      </why>
    </validate>
  </step>

  <step id="fetch">
    <description>Fetch full article content for triaged items</description>

    <precheck>
      <shell>
        count=$(ls box/inbox/news/*.news-item.card 2>/dev/null | wc -l)
        if [ "$count" -eq 0 ]; then
          exit $CHECK_SKIP
        fi
        echo "Found $count items to fetch"
      </shell>
      <why>Nothing to fetch if no items survived triage</why>
    </precheck>

    <run>
      <shell>cb fetch-all-news --dir box/inbox/news</shell>
    </run>
  </step>

  <step id="analyze">
    <description>Analyze fetched articles and move to pool</description>

    <precheck>
      <shell>
        count=$(ls box/inbox/news/*.news-item.card 2>/dev/null | wc -l)
        if [ "$count" -eq 0 ]; then
          exit $CHECK_SKIP
        fi
        echo "Found $count items to analyze"
      </shell>
      <why>Nothing to analyze if no items remain after fetch</why>
    </precheck>

    <run>
      <agent model="sonnet" max-turns="50">
        You are analyzing news articles in a Callback Box.

        YOUR TASK:
        For each news item in box/inbox/news/:
        1. Read the card (it already has &lt;content&gt; with the fetched article)
        2. Create an analysis of how this article fits into the "mental space"
        3. Move the analyzed item to box/pool/news/

        STEP 1 - READ AND ANALYZE:
        Each card already has a &lt;content&gt; element with the fetched article in markdown format.
        Read the content and add an &lt;analysis&gt; element. This is NOT a summary of the content
        (the content is already there). Instead, analyze:

        - Topics: What themes/subjects does it cover?
        - Type: Is it news, opinion, tutorial, announcement, research, etc.?
        - Thesis: If opinion/analysis, what's the main argument?
        - Tone: Measured, urgent, casual, academic, promotional?
        - Timeliness: Is it breaking news, timely, or evergreen?
        - Notes: How might this be used in an edition? What pairs well with it?
        - Questions: Any open questions this raises?

        Example analysis element:
        ```xml
        &lt;analysis analyzed-at="[current ISO timestamp]"&gt;
          &lt;topics&gt;
            &lt;topic&gt;AI safety&lt;/topic&gt;
            &lt;topic&gt;regulation&lt;/topic&gt;
          &lt;/topics&gt;
          &lt;type&gt;opinion&lt;/type&gt;
          &lt;thesis&gt;AI regulation should focus on outcomes rather than methods&lt;/thesis&gt;
          &lt;tone&gt;measured, academic&lt;/tone&gt;
          &lt;timeliness&gt;evergreen&lt;/timeliness&gt;
          &lt;notes&gt;Could pair well with EU AI Act coverage.&lt;/notes&gt;
          &lt;questions&gt;
            &lt;question&gt;How does this compare to the Anthropic safety approach?&lt;/question&gt;
          &lt;/questions&gt;
        &lt;/analysis&gt;
        ```

        If a card has a &lt;fetch-error&gt; instead of &lt;content&gt;, note it in the analysis and
        still move it to pool — the brief can decide whether to include it based on
        title/summary alone or skip it.

        STEP 2 - MOVE TO POOL:
        After adding analysis to ALL items, move them all at once:
        ```
        cb move box/inbox/news/item1.news-item.card box/inbox/news/item2.news-item.card ... box/pool/news/
        ```

        cb move accepts multiple source paths with a directory destination (like mv).
        Do NOT call it once per item — use a single command for all moves.
        Then commit all changes.

        OUTPUT: As you process each item, state what you found:
          ANALYZED: [filename] - [topics/type/timeliness summary]

        COMMIT (REQUIRED — do not skip):
        You MUST commit before finishing. Uncommitted work gets a generic fallback message.

        ```bash
        git add -A &amp;&amp; git commit -m "$(cat &lt;&lt;'EOF'
        Analyze &lt;N&gt; items: &lt;key themes&gt;

        Items:
        - &lt;title&gt; (&lt;type&gt;, &lt;timeliness&gt;) - &lt;topics&gt;

        Emerging themes: &lt;notable patterns or connections&gt;

        Triggered-By: cb workflow
        Phase: analyze
        EOF
        )"
        ```

        GIT: Do NOT add Co-Authored-By to commits. The system adds appropriate trailers automatically.
      </agent>
    </run>

    <validate severity="review">
      <shell>
        remaining=$(ls box/inbox/news/*.news-item.card 2>/dev/null | wc -l)
        echo "Remaining in inbox: $remaining"
        [ "$remaining" -eq 0 ]
      </shell>
      <instruction>
        All items should have been moved from box/inbox/news/ to box/pool/news/
        with an analysis element added to each. Items with fetch-error should
        still be moved and analyzed based on title/summary alone.
      </instruction>
      <why>
        Items left in inbox won't reach the brief. The analyze step must
        process everything that triage kept.
      </why>
    </validate>
  </step>

  <step id="brief">
    <description>Create a narrative news brief from pooled items</description>

    <precheck>
      <shell>
        count=$(ls box/pool/news/*.news-item.card 2>/dev/null | wc -l)
        if [ "$count" -eq 0 ]; then
          exit $CHECK_SKIP
        fi
        echo "Found $count items in pool"
      </shell>
      <why>Can't create a brief without items in the pool</why>
    </precheck>

    <run>
      <agent model="sonnet" max-turns="40">
        You are creating a personal news brief in a Callback Box.

        YOUR ROLE:
        You are a personal assistant curating news for ONE reader. This is not journalism competing
        for attention on a crowded homepage. You don't need to convince anyone to read—they already
        want to. Your job is to serve the reader's genuine interests, not to maximize engagement.

        AVOID:
        - Clickbait headlines ("Your Phone's Secret Conversations", "What They Don't Want You to Know")
        - Urgency and alarm ("Breaking:", "Critical:", manufactured importance)
        - Mystery as hook (hiding the point to create curiosity)
        - Superlatives and hype ("Revolutionary", "Game-changing", "Mind-blowing")
        - Treating speculation as fact

        INSTEAD:
        - Be direct and informative ("New EU AI regulation passes", "How the exploit works")
        - Trust the reader's intelligence and curiosity
        - Let interesting things be interesting on their own merits
        - Summarize accurately rather than tantalizingly
        - Be a knowledgeable friend, not a headline writer

        CRITICAL - PROVIDE FULL CONTEXT:
        The reader has NOT read the source articles. They only see what you write. For each story:
        - Introduce what happened and why it matters BEFORE diving into analysis
        - Include enough background that the piece stands alone
        - Use &lt;excerpt&gt; elements to quote key passages from the source—this provides texture and
          lets the original author's voice come through
        - Don't assume shared context; restate key facts even if they seem obvious

        YOUR TASK:
        Create a news-brief card from items in box/pool/news/. This is a narrative publication,
        not just a list of summaries.

        STEP 0 - READ OR CREATE THE USER GUIDE:
        First, check if config/news-guide.news-guide.card exists. If it does, read it to understand:
        - User interests (with confidence levels)
        - Disinterests (things to avoid)
        - Preferences (depth, tone, etc.)
        - Active experiments to test
        - Context notes that might affect curation

        If the guide exists but has NO active/proposed experiments (all are successful/unsuccessful/inconclusive),
        create 1-2 new experiments based on what you've learned. The guide should always have experiments to run.

        If no guide exists, create one:
        ```
        cb create config/news-guide.news-guide.card
        ```

        Then edit the file, replacing the comment placeholders with actual content inferred from
        the news sources in the pool. The guide MUST follow this schema:

        ```xml
        &lt;news-guide version="1.0.0"&gt;
          &lt;updated-at&gt;[current ISO timestamp]&lt;/updated-at&gt;

          &lt;interests&gt;
            &lt;topic confidence="low" source="inferred"&gt;Topic name here&lt;/topic&gt;
          &lt;/interests&gt;

          &lt;disinterests&gt;
            &lt;disinterest confidence="low" source="inferred"&gt;Thing to avoid&lt;/disinterest&gt;
          &lt;/disinterests&gt;

          &lt;preferences&gt;
            &lt;preference aspect="depth" confidence="low" source="inferred"&gt;
              Description of preference
            &lt;/preference&gt;
          &lt;/preferences&gt;

          &lt;context-notes&gt;
            &lt;context duration="ongoing"&gt;
              Contextual notes about curation situation
            &lt;/context&gt;
          &lt;/context-notes&gt;

          &lt;experiments&gt;
            &lt;experiment id="exp-1" status="proposed"&gt;
              &lt;hypothesis&gt;What you want to test&lt;/hypothesis&gt;
              &lt;approach&gt;How to test it&lt;/approach&gt;
            &lt;/experiment&gt;
          &lt;/experiments&gt;
        &lt;/news-guide&gt;
        ```

        IMPORTANT schema rules:
        - Use &lt;topic&gt; inside &lt;interests&gt;, NOT &lt;interest&gt;
        - confidence must be: confirmed, high, medium, low, or hypothesis
        - source must be: user-stated, feedback, inferred, or default
        - preference aspect must be: depth, tone, frequency, format, sources, or other
        - experiment status must be: proposed, active, successful, unsuccessful, or inconclusive

        STEP 1 - SURVEY THE POOL:
        Read all items in box/pool/news/ with their &lt;analysis&gt; elements.
        Look for:
        - Common themes that connect multiple articles
        - Timely items that should be featured today
        - Interesting contrasts or tensions between pieces
        - A narrative arc that could make this edition compelling
        - Opportunities to test hypotheses from the guide

        Optionally, read recent briefs in store/archive/briefs/ to:
        - Avoid repeating themes too soon
        - Build on ongoing stories
        - Reference previous coverage

        STEP 2 - PLAN THE BRIEF:
        Before writing, decide:
        - What's the headline/angle for this brief?
        - What 2-3 themes or sections will structure it?
        - Which articles are primary (drive the narrative)?
        - Which are supporting (add depth)?
        - What expandos would enhance without cluttering?
        - Are there questions to pose to the reader?

        STEP 3 - CREATE THE BRIEF:
        Create the brief file in box/output/briefs/ (NOT inbox - briefs are output, not incoming items):
        ```
        cb create box/output/briefs/&lt;date&gt;_&lt;slug&gt;.news-brief.card
        ```

        Then edit it with this structure. IMPORTANT: &lt;curation&gt; comes FIRST because editorial
        decisions should be made before writing content:

        ```xml
        &lt;news-brief&gt;
          &lt;curation guide-version="[timestamp from guide's updated-at]"&gt;
            &lt;interest application="featured"&gt;Topic from guide that was featured&lt;/interest&gt;
            &lt;interest application="tested"&gt;Topic being tested as hypothesis&lt;/interest&gt;
            &lt;experiment-ref id="exp-id"&gt;How this edition tests the experiment&lt;/experiment-ref&gt;
            &lt;hypothesis id="h1" experiment-ref="exp-id"&gt;
              Specific testable claim about what will work
            &lt;/hypothesis&gt;
            &lt;rationale&gt;
              Brief explanation of editorial choices and why this angle/approach was selected.
            &lt;/rationale&gt;
          &lt;/curation&gt;

          &lt;title&gt;Compelling headline that captures the theme&lt;/title&gt;
          &lt;date&gt;[today's date YYYY-MM-DD]&lt;/date&gt;
          &lt;byline&gt;One sentence teaser of what's inside&lt;/byline&gt;

          &lt;content format="markdown"&gt;
        The opening paragraph sets up the theme.

        &lt;section id="s1" heading="First Theme" link="https://example.com/article" via="Hacker News"&gt;

        **Start with context**: What is this about? Who did what?

        &lt;excerpt source="Article Title" link="https://example.com/article"&gt;
        A memorable passage from the article.
        &lt;/excerpt&gt;

        &lt;expando title="Deep dive: Technical details" id="exp1"&gt;
        More detailed content that interested readers can explore.
        &lt;/expando&gt;

        &lt;/section&gt;

        &lt;section id="s2" heading="Second Theme" link="https://example.com/other" via="Lobsters"&gt;

        Establish context first. Then your narrative...

        &lt;query id="q1" prompt="What aspects of this interest you most?"&gt;
        Understanding your interests helps focus future coverage.
        &lt;/query&gt;

        &lt;/section&gt;

        Closing thoughts.
          &lt;/content&gt;

          &lt;sources&gt;
            &lt;source path="store/archive/news/Article_One.news-item.card" usage="primary"&gt;Article Title&lt;/source&gt;
          &lt;/sources&gt;
        &lt;/news-brief&gt;
        ```

        CURATION NOTES:
        - &lt;curation&gt; comes FIRST - decide what to write about before writing
        - Include interests that drove content selection
        - Reference active experiments being tested
        - State hypotheses that feedback can confirm/deny
        - The rationale explains your editorial thinking

        GUIDELINES:
        - EVERY SECTION needs: link= to the source article, via= to note the feed source
        - Include &lt;excerpt&gt; elements liberally—at least one per section, more in expandos
        - The &lt;feed&gt; element in each news-item card contains the feed title (use this for "via")
        - The &lt;link&gt; element in each news-item card has the article URL
        - Provide full context: the reader knows NOTHING except what you write
        - BE LIBERAL with expandos - long is fine if it's expandable
        - The main narrative should be scannable (1-2 paragraphs per section)
        - Use expandos for: technical details, background, quotes, tangents
        - Use queries to invite reader engagement (but sparingly)
        - Quote sources directly when they say it better
        - Link themes across articles, don't just list them

        STEP 4 - ARCHIVE USED ITEMS:
        After creating the brief, move ALL used items to archive in one command:
        ```
        cb move box/pool/news/&lt;used1&gt; box/pool/news/&lt;used2&gt; ... store/archive/news/
        ```

        cb move accepts multiple source paths with a directory destination.
        This handles moving and updating any references (including in the brief you just created).
        Leave items in pool that weren't used—they'll be available for future briefs.

        STEP 5 - CREATE NOTIFICATION:
        After creating the brief and archiving items, create a notification command card
        so the user gets alerted in their browser. First create the card:
        ```
        cb create box/commands/notify-brief_&lt;date&gt;.open-tab.command.card
        ```
        Then edit it to contain:
        ```xml
        &lt;command type="open-tab" status="ready"&gt;
          &lt;url&gt;http://localhost:3210/news/box/output/briefs/&lt;brief-filename&gt;&lt;/url&gt;
          &lt;title&gt;&lt;brief title&gt;&lt;/title&gt;
          &lt;message&gt;Your news brief is ready&lt;/message&gt;
        &lt;/command&gt;
        ```
        Replace &lt;brief-filename&gt; with the actual filename of the brief you just created,
        and &lt;brief title&gt; with the title you chose for the brief.

        COMMIT (REQUIRED — do not skip):
        You MUST commit before finishing. Uncommitted work gets a generic fallback message.

        ```bash
        git add -A &amp;&amp; git commit -m "$(cat &lt;&lt;'EOF'
        Brief: &lt;title&gt;

        Sections:
        - &lt;section heading&gt; (&lt;source article title&gt;)

        &lt;N&gt; items used, &lt;M&gt; left in pool.
        Editorial angle: &lt;one sentence on why this angle/structure&gt;

        Triggered-By: cb workflow
        Phase: brief
        EOF
        )"
        ```

        GIT: Do NOT add Co-Authored-By to commits. The system adds appropriate trailers automatically.
      </agent>
    </run>

    <validate severity="review">
      <shell>
        ls box/output/briefs/*.news-brief.card 2>/dev/null
        # Validate that all brief cards parse as valid XML
        for f in box/output/briefs/*.news-brief.card; do
          if [ -f "$f" ]; then
            cb validate "$f" || { echo "INVALID XML: $f"; exit 1; }
          fi
        done
      </shell>
      <instruction>
        A new brief should exist in box/output/briefs/ with today's date.
        Items used in the brief should be archived to store/archive/news/.
        Unused items should remain in box/pool/news/ for future briefs.
        The brief's sources should reference all used items.
        All briefs must be valid XML (no unescaped ampersands, etc.).
      </instruction>
      <why>
        The brief is the whole point of the pipeline. If it wasn't created,
        or if used items weren't archived, the pipeline didn't complete its job.
        Invalid XML means the brief can't be displayed to the user.
      </why>
    </validate>
  </step>

  <step id="execute">
    <description>Execute any pending command cards (e.g. notifications)</description>

    <precheck>
      <shell>
        count=$(ls box/commands/*.command.card 2>/dev/null | wc -l)
        if [ "$count" -eq 0 ]; then
          exit $CHECK_SKIP
        fi
        echo "Found $count command(s) to execute"
      </shell>
      <why>Nothing to execute if no command cards exist</why>
    </precheck>

    <run>
      <shell>cb execute-commands</shell>
    </run>
  </step>
</workflow>
